  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nThe next round of showers and thunderstorms with more heavy rainfall\nis currently tracking into our counties along the Ohio and Indiana \nstate line near the Richmond and Greenville area. This is the next\nwave or disturbance that is riding along the stalled frontal \nboundary over our area. The latest 00z CAM high-res forecast guidance\nindicate this complex of strong to severe storms may evolve into a\nslow moving MCS as it tracks southeastward across our area this\nmorning. SPC continues to have much of the CWA in a marginal to \nslight severe risk through this morning with damaging winds as the \nmain threat. Marginal severe hail and an isolated brief tornado is \nalso possible. Heavy rainfall rates and training of convection will\nlead to additional flash flooding concerns. \n\nThere will be a decent lull in the organized convection late this \nmorning into the afternoon. Rain chances will lower later today. But\nthere is some uncertainty on the forecast going into tonight. Some of\nthe forecast model guidance indicates the potential for another \nround of showers and storms that may develop again late tonight into\nearly Saturday morning to our northwest and track southeastward into\nparts of the area. The current flood watch is through 15z this\nmorning. We will take another look at the new 12z forecast guidance\nand may have to extend portions of the flood watch if trends show\nanother round of convection.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nA small mid level ridge of will try to build northward into the lower\nOhio Valley this weekend. This ridge will shift the stationary\nfrontal boundary and active storm track of convection northward into\nthe lower Great Lakes and Upper Ohio Valley region Saturday into\nSunday. Warmer temperatures and a very humid air mass will lead to\nhigh temperatures in the upper 80s and lower 90s this weekend. Heat\nindices may approach advisory criteria between 100 and 104 degrees\nSaturday and Sunday. A cold front will push southeastward through \nthe Ohio Valley Sunday night into Monday morning with another round\nof showers and thunderstorms. A weak area of high pressure will build\ndown across the Great Lakes region and Ohio Valley Monday and Tuesday\nwith slightly cooler temperatures and lower humidity.",
